        yearRange: "1994-1996",
        timingType: "Chain (Located at the FRONT of the engine. The M111 utilizes an incredibly robust duplex (double-row) timing chain. Unlike modern engines, this timing system is massively over-engineered and practically indestructible. It rarely requires replacement or even servicing before 250,000 miles (400,000 km), provided basic oil change intervals are respected.)",
        injectionType: "Manifold Injection / Bosch HFM (Hot-Film Mass Air Flow). Unlike the disastrous PMS system used on the early 1.8L and 2.0L models globally, the North American 2.2L C 220 was blessed with the infinitely superior Bosch HFM system from day one. It utilizes a traditional Mass Air Flow sensor, making it vastly more reliable, easier to diagnose, and completely immune to the fatal internal MAP sensor meltdowns that killed the smaller engines.)",
        engineLifespan: "300,000+ Miles / 500,000+ km (Mechanically, the 2.2L M111 cast-iron engine block is legendary. Because it avoids the horrific PMS engine management system, the powertrain is incredibly reliable. The engine will easily outlast the chassis. However, the economic lifespan of this pre-facelift model is still heavily threatened by the infamous 'biodegradable' engine wiring harness and severe structural chassis rust, even in dry climates.)",
        turboType: "Naturally Aspirated (No turbocharger or supercharger. Power delivery is extremely linear, smooth, and highly durable. The 2.2L displacement provides excellent mid-range torque, making it a capable commuter for American highways, though not a sports sedan.)",
        ecuType: "Bosch HFM (A massive leap forward in reliability. It separately manages fuel injection and ignition using standard sensors. This specific USA/Canada variant has an early OBD-I (and transitionary OBD-II in 1996) emissions map, which slightly detunes the peak horsepower to 147hp to meet federal standards. While tuning potential is essentially zero on the naturally aspirated 2.2L, the ECU is rock-solid.)",
        euroStandard: "US EPA Tier 1 / OBD-I (OBD-II compliant for 1996 model year)",
        maxPowerStockInternals: "350+ hp (The iron block and forged internals of the 2.2L M111 are famously overbuilt. The bottom end can handle massive boost. However, tuning the Bosch HFM natively yields almost no gains. Adding forced induction requires a standalone ECU like Megasquirt, which is popular in the European tuning scene but rare in North America for this chassis.)",
        fuelConsumption: {
          city: "11.5 - 13.5 L/100km (20-24 MPG)",
          highway: "7.5 - 9.0 L/100km (26-31 MPG)",
          mixed: "9.5 - 11.0 L/100km (22-26 MPG)",
          sporty: "14.0+ L/100km"
        },
        oilSpecification: "10W-40 or 5W-40 Synthetic/Semi-Synthetic (MB 229.1 or MB 229.3. Tolerates thicker traditional oils very well in hot US climates.)",
        commonIssues: [
          "Biodegradable Engine Wiring Harness: THE absolute most critical flaw. Between 1994 and 1996, Mercedes used eco-friendly wire insulation that turns to dust from engine heat. The exposed copper wires touch, causing massive short circuits that instantly destroy the electronic throttle body and ignition coils. If the harness hasn't been rebuilt yet, it is guaranteed to fail.",
          "W202 Chassis Rust: The pre-facelift W202 C-Class is notorious for catastrophic rust, particularly around the front spring perches (which can snap off while driving), wheel arches, and trunk lock. Even cars from dry US states can suffer spring perch failure due to trapped moisture.",
          "MAF Sensor Failure: The Bosch HFM system relies entirely on the Hot-Film Mass Air Flow sensor. Over time, the platinum wire becomes contaminated with dirt or oil vapor, causing rough idles, hesitation, and poor fuel economy. Requires replacement with a genuine Bosch unit.",
          "Head Gasket Oil Leak: The M111 almost universally develops an external oil leak at the right-rear corner of the cylinder head (passenger side, near the firewall). This is an external oil weep and rarely mixes oil with coolant, but eventually requires a head gasket replacement.",
          "Electronic Throttle Body (ETA) Wiring: The VDO electronic throttle body also uses the biodegradable wiring internally. As the wires short out, the car loses idle control, suffers from surging, and drops into Limp Mode.",
          "Vacuum Leaks & Brittle Plastic: Given the age of the vehicle and the heat of the engine bay, the plastic vacuum lines controlling the HVAC system and engine ancillaries frequently snap, causing rough idles and climate control issues."
        ],
        vulnerabilities: [
          "Economic Total Loss (Wiring Harness Destruction): If the biodegradable wiring harness crumbles and shorts out, it will fry the ignition coils and the electronic throttle body simultaneously. Combining a custom rebuilt wiring harness with a new ETA often exceeds the total value of the car in the US market.",
          "Catastrophic Suspension Failure (Spring Perches): The front suspension spring perches are spot-welded to the chassis and are highly prone to rusting completely through. The spring can violently detach from the car while driving, dropping the suspension onto the tire. This is a severe safety hazard.",
          "Engine Misfire / Coil Degradation: Running old, worn-out spark plugs increases electrical resistance, which damages the waste-spark ignition coils. A failed coil will cause severe misfires and unburnt fuel to dump into the exhaust, destroying the catalytic converter.",
          "Overheating (Viscous Fan Clutch): The cooling system relies on a mechanical viscous fan clutch. As the fluid inside leaks out over the decades, the fan stops engaging at high temperatures, causing the engine to rapidly overheat in heavy traffic.",
          "Evaporator Core Leaks: While not strictly an engine issue, the W202 is notorious for leaking A/C evaporator cores buried deep inside the dashboard. Repairing this requires a 15+ hour dashboard-out labor job, often totaling the car economically."
        ],
