    timingType: "Chain (Located at the FRONT of the engine block. The M113 AMG utilizes an incredibly robust duplex (double-row) timing chain that is heavily over-engineered, standing as a testament to Mercedes-Benz's dedication to longevity. The M113's timing system is phenomenally durable. The chain runs on heavily reinforced plastic guide rails and is kept under optimal tension by a hydraulic tensioner. If the owner strictly adheres to 10 000 km oil change intervals with high-quality MB 229.5 synthetic oil, this timing chain is a true 'lifetime' component that will rarely, if ever, require servicing or replacement before the engine reaches 400 000 to 500 000 kilometers.)",
    injectionType: "Manifold Injection / Bosch ME 2.8 (This engine features a fully electronic, highly advanced sequential fuel injection system managed by the Bosch ME 2.8 architecture. The ME 2.8 system utilizes a highly accurate Hot-Film Mass Air Flow (MAF) sensor to measure the massive volume of incoming air required by the 5.4L V8. The system utilizes modern Bosch solenoid injectors that provide excellent fuel atomization. While the injection system itself is incredibly reliable and virtually never suffers from wiring shorts, it is extremely sensitive to vacuum leaks originating from the aging PCV breather hoses beneath the engine cover.)",
    engineLifespan: "400 000 - 500 000+ km (The M113 V8 is universally celebrated by Mercedes-Benz mechanics and AMG enthusiasts as the most reliable, durable, and trouble-free V8 engine the company has ever manufactured in its long history. The engine utilizes a lightweight but incredibly rigid aluminum block featuring advanced silicon-aluminum (Silitec) cylinder liners, which provide exceptionally low friction and incredible wear resistance. Because this naturally aspirated 5.4-liter variant avoids the immense heat and pressure of the supercharged M113K engines, the forged internal rotating assembly is almost completely unstressed. The engine will effortlessly outlast the W203 chassis it sits in.)",
    turboType: "Naturally Aspirated (The engine relies entirely on atmospheric pressure, completely lacking any turbocharger or supercharger. Instead of forced induction, AMG engineers focused on maximizing displacement and optimizing the cylinder head flow to provide a beautifully smooth, relentlessly muscular torque curve. The power delivery is exceptionally linear, refined, and incredibly aggressive when pushed, entirely avoiding the intercooler and supercharger clutch failures that plagued the earlier C 32 AMG.)",
    ecuType: "Bosch ME 2.8 (A rock-solid, highly sophisticated engine management system that acts as the electronic brain of the entire powertrain. It is deeply and flawlessly integrated with the heavily reinforced AMG SpeedShift 5G-Tronic (722.6) automatic transmission, constantly communicating via a CAN-bus network to deliver incredibly fast shifts. While the ECU module itself is virtually indestructible, it is entirely reliant on the Crankshaft Position Sensor (CPS). This CPS is infamous for degrading due to engine heat; when the sensor fails, it loses its magnetic signal, causing the ECU to abruptly shut off fuel and spark, instantly stalling the engine in traffic.)",
    euroStandard: "Euro 4",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "550+ hp (The internal rotating assembly of the M113 is vastly overbuilt, featuring a robust forged steel crankshaft, heavy-duty connecting rods, and high-quality cast aluminum pistons that can withstand significant cylinder pressures. The most common and spectacular modification for the C 55 is retrofitting the factory Eaton supercharger from the E 55 AMG (creating a custom M113K). The stock naturally aspirated bottom end handles the forced induction brilliantly, instantly unlocking 500+ horsepower.)",
