    cylinders: 4,
    valves: 16,
    compression: "18.0:1",
    yearRange: "2000-2003",
    timingType: "Chain (Located at the FRONT of the engine block. The OM611 utilizes an incredibly robust duplex (double-row) timing chain. Unlike modern lightweight diesel engines, this timing system is massively over-engineered. It is a true 'lifetime' component that will rarely, if ever, require servicing or replacement before the engine reaches 400 000 to 500 000 kilometers, provided basic 10 000 km oil change intervals with high-quality synthetic oil are strictly respected. The chain guides are robust, though the hydraulic chain tensioner can occasionally stick at extreme mileages, causing a brief cold-start rattle.)",
    injectionType: "Common Rail Direct Injection / Bosch EDC15C6 (This early Common Rail system utilizes an engine-driven high-pressure pump (CP1) that pressurizes a single fuel rail up to 1 350 bar. Bosch solenoid injectors deliver precise bursts of fuel directly into the combustion chambers. While incredibly efficient and smooth, this early CDI system is plagued by the infamous 'Black Death'—a critical failure where the copper crush washers sealing the injectors to the cylinder head fail, allowing combustion gases and tar-like diesel sludge to bury the injectors permanently.)",
    engineLifespan: "400 000 - 500 000+ km (The mechanical core of the OM611 cast-iron engine block is legendary for its durability. Because this specific C 220 CDI variant is heavily over-engineered, the internal rotating assembly, turbocharger, and cooling system are completely unstressed. The engine will effortlessly outlast the W203 chassis. The true enemies of this vehicle's lifespan are catastrophic structural rust on the bodywork, the 'Black Death' injector seal failure destroying the cylinder head, and electrical gremlins in the early W203 platform.)",
    turboType: "Turbocharged (Equipped with a Garrett GT1749V Variable Nozzle Turbine (VNT) turbocharger. The variable vanes adjust exhaust gas flow to spool the turbo instantly off idle, entirely eliminating traditional turbo lag and providing peak torque at a very low 1 800 RPM. The turbo operates well below its thermal limits. However, the variable vanes are highly prone to sticking if the car is driven exclusively in stop-and-go city traffic, causing underboost codes and Limp Mode.)",
    ecuType: "Bosch EDC15C6 (A highly sophisticated and reliable electronic diesel control module. It perfectly integrates common rail pressure monitoring, VNT turbo actuation, and the electronic EGR valve. The ECU is fully OBD-II compliant and incredibly robust. The ECU is incredibly receptive to software flashing, allowing tuners to easily unlock massive performance gains via the diagnostic port.)",
    euroStandard: "Euro 3",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "220+ hp (The heavy cast-iron block and forged internals of the OM611 are vastly overbuilt. The limiting factor for horsepower is the small stock GT1749V turbocharger and the flow capacity of the standard Bosch injectors. The bottom end can easily handle over 2.0 bar of boost. Upgrading to a larger turbocharger from the OM612 2.7L engine and fitting larger injector nozzles unlocks immense power without stressing the rods.)",
    fuelConsumption: {
      city: "8.0 - 9.0 L/100km",
      highway: "5.2 - 6.2 L/100km",
      mixed: "6.5 - 7.5 L/100km",
      sporty: "9.0+ L/100km"
    },
    oilSpecification: "6.0L 5W-40 Synthetic (MB 229.3 or MB 229.5 strictly required)",
    commonIssues: [
      "Injector Seal Failure (Black Death): The absolute most critical flaw. The tiny copper washer sealing the fuel injector to the cylinder head degrades. High-pressure combustion gas escapes, baking raw diesel fuel into a solid, black, tar-like rock that completely buries the injectors and valve cover.",
      "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) Leaks: The Bosch CP1 pump is driven by the camshaft. Its internal rubber O-rings degrade over time, causing it to leak diesel fuel externally down the front of the engine block, leading to a loss of rail pressure and hard starting.",
      "EGR Valve & Intake Manifold Clogging: The Exhaust Gas Recirculation system constantly pumps sticky soot back into the intake manifold. Over 100 000 kilometers, this creates a thick, restrictive sludge that chokes airflow entirely.",
      "Rear SAM Water Intrusion: The rear Signal Acquisition Module located in the trunk is highly vulnerable. If the trunk seals fail, water floods the module, causing bizarre electrical shorts.",
      "722.6 Automatic Transmission 13-Pin Connector Leak: The 5-speed electronic transmission frequently leaks ATF from the 13-pin pilot bushing. Capillary action draws the fluid up the wiring harness directly into the Transmission Control Module (TCM).",
      "W203 Chassis Rust: Catastrophic structural rust around the spot-welded front spring perches, jacking points, wheel arches, and trunk lid."
    ],
    vulnerabilities: [
      "Catastrophic Engine Damage (Black Death): Ignoring the hissing sound of a leaking injector seal will result in the injector fusing permanently to the aluminum cylinder head, requiring the entire head to be removed and machined at immense cost.",
      "Transmission Control Module Destruction: Capillary action from a leaking 13-pin connector on the 722.6 gearbox will push ATF into the passenger footwell, soaking and permanently destroying the expensive TCM.",
      "Catastrophic Suspension Failure (Spring Perches): The front spring perches are spot-welded to the chassis and are highly prone to rusting completely through. The heavy coil spring can violently detach, dropping the suspension.",
      "Economic Total Loss (Rear SAM Failure): Water damage to the rear SAM module requires expensive component replacement and complex dealership re-coding, often costing upwards of 1 000 EUR.",
      "Turbocharger Seizure (VNT Vanes): Continuous low-speed urban driving builds up hard carbon inside the turbine housing, permanently jamming the variable vanes. This triggers continuous overboost or underboost Limp Modes.",
      "Economic Total Loss (Rust): Severe structural rust on the subframe mounts and chassis legs frequently costs significantly more to professionally repair than the actual market value of the vehicle."
    ],
