    injectionType: "Common Rail Direct Injection / Bosch EDC16CP31 (A highly advanced, ultra-high-pressure Generation 3 Common Rail system operating at 1 600 bar via a Bosch CP3 pump. It utilizes lightning-fast piezo injectors capable of multiple precisely timed injections per stroke. This entirely eradicates traditional diesel clatter, making the V6 sound incredibly smooth and refined. However, these piezo injectors are extremely sensitive to poor fuel quality and are astronomically expensive to replace when they fail.)",
    engineLifespan: "300 000 - 450 000+ km (The core aluminum block with cast-iron cylinder liners is incredibly robust. However, the lifespan of the OM642 is entirely dictated by the owner's willingness to address profound oil leak issues in the engine valley and complex emission control system failures. This specific variant is paired with a manual transmission, which is severely punished by the V6's torque, leading to frequent and expensive dual-mass flywheel replacements. If the oil cooler seals are upgraded and the intake tract kept clean, the engine itself will run for a very long time.)",
    turboType: "Turbocharged (Equipped with an advanced, electronically actuated Garrett VNT turbocharger mounted directly in the center 'V' of the engine block. The electronic actuator provides incredibly precise boost control, entirely eliminating turbo lag. However, the turbocharger's intake seal is globally infamous for degrading and dripping oily PCV vapor directly onto the electronic swirl flap actuator motor located directly beneath it, causing catastrophic Limp Mode failures.)",
    ecuType: "Bosch EDC16CP31 (A highly sophisticated electronic diesel control module that manages the piezo injectors, VNT turbo, EGR, and factory DPF systems. This specific ECU mapping is incredibly unique: it artificially restricts the massive OM642 engine to just 415 Nm of torque (down from the automatic's 510 Nm) specifically to prevent the 6-speed manual transmission and dual-mass flywheel from instantly shredding themselves under acceleration.)",
