    timingType: "Chain (Located at the FRONT of the engine block. The M271 CGI evolved from the Kompressor engines but fatally retained the disastrous single-row (simplex) timing chain design. This simplex chain is highly prone to stretching prematurely due to the stress of the advanced variable valve timing system and turbocharger load. When the chain stretches, it destroys the teeth on the immensely expensive Variable Valve Timing (VVT) camshaft adjusters. If the telltale metallic cold-start rattle is ignored, the chain will jump teeth, causing catastrophic piston-to-valve contact. It MUST be replaced preventatively every 80 000 to 100 000 kilometers along with the tensioner.)",
    injectionType: "Direct Injection / Siemens SIM271 (A highly complex, ultra-high-pressure gasoline direct injection system. It utilizes an engine-driven High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) that injects fuel directly into the combustion chamber rather than the manifold. While this provides excellent efficiency and low-end torque, it introduces massive vulnerabilities. The HPFP frequently leaks internally, diluting the engine oil with raw gasoline and wiping out engine bearings. Furthermore, because fuel no longer washes over the intake valves, the CGI system suffers from severe, performance-robbing carbon buildup on the intake valves, requiring physical walnut blasting to clean.)",
    engineLifespan: "200 000 - 300 000+ km (The mechanical lifespan of the all-aluminum M271 EVO (CGI) block is entirely dictated by the owner's maintenance vigilance. If the timing chain and camshaft gears are replaced exactly when needed, and the engine oil is constantly monitored for gasoline dilution from the HPFP, the bottom end will last a long time. However, the immense complexity of the direct injection system makes this variant significantly more fragile, temperamental, and expensive to maintain than the older Kompressor models. Neglected chains or fuel pumps will instantly destroy this engine.)",
    turboType: "Turbocharged (Equipped with an exhaust-driven turbocharger, completely replacing the Eaton M45 superchargers of the previous generation. This transition significantly improved fuel efficiency and lowered parasitic drag on the engine. The small turbocharger spools very quickly, providing 250 Nm of torque at incredibly low RPMs, mimicking the feel of a larger naturally aspirated engine. However, the turbocharger brings its own issues, notably wastegate rattle and oil feed line leaks over high mileages.)",
    ecuType: "Siemens SIM271 (A highly complex and strict engine management system designed to tightly control the extremely narrow operating windows of direct injection. The ECU actively monitors rail pressure and precisely maps injector pulse widths. Like the older M271 engines, the ECU remains vulnerable to oil intrusion from the engine wiring harness. If oil reaches the ECU pins from the leaking camshaft adjuster magnets, the entire unit will short out and require extremely expensive replacement.)",
    euroStandard: "Euro 5",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "260+ hp (The internal rotating assembly of the 1.8-liter M271 CGI features lowered 9.3:1 compression specifically to handle turbo boost. Because this C 180 CGI shares identical physical hardware with the 204 horsepower C 250 CGI, the stock internals are vastly overbuilt for its 156hp rating. The true limitation for massive power is the flow capacity of the factory turbocharger and the strict flow limits of the direct fuel injectors.)",
