    yearRange: "2007-2009",
    timingType: "Chain (Located at the FRONT of the engine block. The M272 utilizes a double-row timing chain. Unlike the catastrophic single-row chains of the 4-cylinder engines, this chain is generally incredibly robust. However, early M272 engines were globally infamous for a fatal flaw where the balance shaft chain sprocket was manufactured with insufficiently hardened steel, causing it to wear down prematurely and destroy the timing. Since the W204 was introduced in 2007, early C 280 models *may* fall into the defective serial number range. It is absolutely critical to verify the engine serial number before purchase. If it falls outside the range, it is a lifetime component.)",
    injectionType: "Manifold Injection / Bosch ME 9.7 (A highly sophisticated sequential electronic fuel injection system. It utilizes modern electronic throttle control and a highly sensitive Hot-Film Mass Air Flow (MAF) sensor. The system is extremely reliable and completely avoids the capillary oil wicking issues that plagued the 4-cylinder engines, as well as the severe carbon buildup issues inherent to later direct-injected CGI engines. However, the variable-length intake manifold uses fragile plastic swirl flap arms that frequently break and trigger performance issues.)",
    engineLifespan: "300 000 - 450 000+ km (The mechanical lifespan of the all-aluminum M272 V6 engine block in the W204 chassis is heavily bifurcated. If the engine falls into the defective balance shaft serial number range and hasn't been fixed, it will self-destruct prematurely. If it has the updated gear, it is an incredibly robust, deeply refined V6. The W204 chassis completely eradicated the structural rust issues of the past. The engine will easily outlast the vehicle with basic maintenance, provided the intake manifold swirl flaps and the thermostat are addressed when they fail.)",
    turboType: "Naturally Aspirated (No turbocharger. The M272 relies entirely on its 3.0-liter displacement, high 11.3:1 compression ratio, and continuously variable valve timing on all four camshafts to generate its incredibly smooth power delivery. Because it lacks forced induction, it completely avoids complex supercharger or turbocharger maintenance issues, making the engine bay significantly simpler and highly reliable.)",
    ecuType: "Bosch ME 9.7 (A highly advanced, lightning-fast engine management system designed to tightly control emissions and precisely manage the continuously variable valve timing. The ECU is incredibly robust and mounts securely on top of the engine block. Because this is a naturally aspirated engine mapped close to its natural efficiency limits, tuning potential is practically nonexistent, making the ECU a 'leave it alone' component.)",
    euroStandard: "Euro 4",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "350+ hp (The internal rotating assembly of the 3.0-liter M272 is vastly overbuilt. However, because it is naturally aspirated, extracting any meaningful horsepower is virtually impossible without completely disassembling the engine. The block can theoretically handle forced induction, but doing so is financially and logically absurd when the massive 3.5L M272 V6 bolts right into the same engine bay.)",
    fuelConsumption: {
      city: "13.5 - 15.0 L/100km",
      highway: "7.5 - 8.8 L/100km",
      mixed: "9.8 - 11.0 L/100km",
      sporty: "14.5+ L/100km"
    },
    oilSpecification: "8.0L 5W-40 Synthetic (MB 229.3 or MB 229.5 strictly required.)",
    commonIssues: [
      "Balance Shaft Gear Wear (Early Models): THE absolute most critical flaw on pre-2008 engines. The gear wears out, requiring full engine removal to fix. Must check serial number.",
      "Intake Manifold Swirl Flaps: The variable length intake manifold uses plastic actuator arms. The heat cycles make the plastic brittle, causing the arms to snap off. This requires an aluminum arm repair kit.",
      "Electronic Steering Lock (ESL/ELV) Failure: THE defining chassis flaw of the W204. The tiny electric motor inside the steering column lock burns out, completely immobilizing the vehicle. The key turns, but the car will not crank.",
      "Camshaft Adjuster Magnets: The magnets on the front of the cylinder heads fail frequently, causing timing error codes. Fortunately, they are cheap and very easy to replace.",
      "7G-Tronic (722.9) Conductor Plate: The 7-speed transmission frequently suffers from speed sensor failures on the internal conductor plate, locking the car in Limp Mode.",
      "Thermostat Failure: The electronically controlled thermostat frequently fails open, preventing the engine from reaching operating temperature."
    ],
    vulnerabilities: [
      "Catastrophic Engine Damage (Balance Shaft): If the balance shaft gear wears down completely, the timing chain will skip, causing catastrophic piston-to-valve contact and destroying the engine.",
      "Immobilization (ESL/ELV Failure): A burnt-out steering lock motor will leave you completely stranded without warning. Repairing this at the dealership is highly expensive; aftermarket emulators offer a cheaper fix.",
      "Transmission Limp Mode (Conductor Plate): A failed speed sensor in the 7G-Tronic transmission requires dropping the valve body and sending the conductor plate out for highly specialized reprogramming.",
      "Intake Manifold Failure: Snapped swirl flap arms will cause the engine to idle terribly, lose massive amounts of low-end torque, and throw permanent Check Engine lights.",
      "Oil Leak Fire Hazard: The PCV centrifuge cover leaking oil onto the catalytic converters creates a terrible burning smell and a potential fire risk if left completely unchecked."
    ],
