    compression: "11.3:1",
    yearRange: "2007-2014",
    timingType: "Chain (Located at the FRONT of the engine block. The M156 utilizes an incredibly robust duplex (double-row) timing chain that is virtually indestructible. However, the true fatal flaw of the timing and valvetrain system lies elsewhere: the camshafts and the hydraulic lifters. Early M156 engines suffer from camshaft lobe wear due to improper metallurgy and inadequate lubrication to the top end. Furthermore, the notorious M156 cylinder head bolts stretch and snap under the immense pressure of the 6.2L V8, allowing engine coolant to mix directly with the engine oil, catastrophically destroying the engine. Replacing the factory head bolts with the updated M157-style bolts is an absolute, non-negotiable requirement for pre-2011 engines.)",
    injectionType: "Manifold Injection / Bosch ME 9.7 AMG (A highly sophisticated sequential electronic fuel injection system mapped specifically by AMG. Unlike modern AMG engines, the M156 uses traditional port injection rather than direct injection. This was a deliberate choice to allow the massive 6.2-liter engine to breathe freely and rev to its 7,200 RPM redline without the limitations of early direct injection systems. Because it uses port injection, the M156 completely avoids the severe carbon buildup issues that plague modern engines, making the intake tract virtually maintenance-free. The fuel delivery is brutally responsive.)",
    engineLifespan: "150 000 - 300 000+ km (The mechanical lifespan of the M156 is entirely bifurcated by the head bolt issue. If a pre-2011 engine has NOT had its head bolts replaced with the updated design, it is a ticking time bomb that will eventually hydrolock or spin a bearing from coolant-diluted oil. If the head bolts are updated, the lifters replaced with SLS AMG 'black series' buckets, and the oil changed religiously every 5 000 km, the core engine block is remarkably robust. The engine consumes oil by design, so running it low will immediately destroy the soft camshafts.)",
    turboType: "Naturally Aspirated (No turbocharger. The M156 relies entirely on its gargantuan 6.2-liter displacement, high 11.3:1 compression ratio, and aggressive camshaft profiles to generate power. Because it lacks forced induction, it delivers an utterly visceral, instantaneous throttle response that modern turbocharged AMG engines simply cannot replicate. The power delivery is violently linear, building into a thunderous crescendo at 7,200 RPM.)",
    ecuType: "Bosch ME 9.7 AMG (A highly advanced, lightning-fast engine management system completely bespoke to AMG. To protect the W204 chassis from the sheer brutality of the 6.2L V8, AMG software-restricted the throttle bodies from opening 100% in the C 63, capping it at 457hp (while the exact same engine made 525hp in the E 63). Because of this artificial electronic restriction, the tuning potential of the C 63 via a simple OBD-II flash is genuinely staggering.)",
    euroStandard: "Euro 4 / Euro 5",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "600+ hp (The internal rotating assembly of the 6.2-liter M156 is vastly overbuilt. A simple ECU tune instantly unlocks 500+ horsepower by allowing the throttle bodies to open fully. With long-tube headers and a tune, the engine comfortably pushes 540+ hp naturally aspirated. Bolting on an aftermarket supercharger kit (e.g., Weistec) can easily push the stock block past 650+ hp, though transmission clutch packs become the limiting factor.)",
