    timingType: "Chain (Located at the FRONT of the engine block. The M156 utilizes an incredibly robust duplex (double-row) timing chain. Because the Edition 507 was released at the very end of the M156 production run, it inherently benefits from the revised M157-style head bolts right from the factory, completely eliminating the catastrophic head bolt snapping issue of early M156s. However, the true fatal flaw of the valvetrain remains: the camshafts and the hydraulic lifters. While the Edition 507 incorporates hardened SLS AMG lifters, the camshaft lobes are still prone to wear if oil intervals are neglected or the car is repeatedly driven aggressively before reaching proper oil temperature.)",
    injectionType: "Manifold Injection / Bosch ME 9.7 AMG (A highly sophisticated sequential electronic fuel injection system mapped specifically by AMG for the Edition 507 to deliver 507 horsepower. Unlike modern AMG engines, the M156 uses traditional port injection rather than direct injection. This was a deliberate choice to allow the massive 6.2-liter engine to breathe freely and rev aggressively to its 7,200 RPM redline. Because it uses port injection, the M156 completely avoids the severe carbon buildup issues that plague modern direct-injected engines, making the intake tract virtually maintenance-free. The fuel delivery is violently responsive.)",
    engineLifespan: "200 000 - 350 000+ km (The mechanical lifespan of the Edition 507 engine is excellent compared to early M156 variants, primarily because the catastrophic head bolt issue was resolved at the factory. Furthermore, the Edition 507 features the forged internal rotating assembly from the SLS AMG supercar, shedding 3 kilograms of rotating mass. This vastly reduces stress on the bottom end at high RPMs. If the oil is changed religiously every 5 000 km to protect the camshafts, the core engine block is virtually indestructible. The engine consumes oil by design, so running it low will immediately destroy the top end.)",
    turboType: "Naturally Aspirated (No turbocharger. The Edition 507 relies entirely on its gargantuan 6.2-liter displacement, high 11.3:1 compression ratio, and aggressive camshaft profiles to generate power. Because it lacks forced induction, it delivers an utterly visceral, instantaneous throttle response that modern turbocharged AMG engines simply cannot replicate. The SLS AMG lightweight forged rotating assembly makes the power delivery violently aggressive and exceptionally rev-happy, screaming toward the redline.)",
    ecuType: "Bosch ME 9.7 AMG (A highly advanced, lightning-fast engine management system completely bespoke to AMG. In the Edition 507, the ECU mapping is specifically revised by AMG to fully utilize the SLS internal hardware, raising the output to 507 horsepower natively. This pushes the M156 much closer to its natural maximum limit without aftermarket modifications, though tuners can still optimize spark timing and fueling for minor gains.)",
    euroStandard: "Euro 5",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "650+ hp (The internal rotating assembly of the Edition 507 is genuinely race-grade. Featuring authentic SLS AMG forged pistons, lightweight connecting rods, and a strengthened crankshaft, the bottom end is immensely strong. With long-tube headers and a tune, the engine comfortably pushes 560+ hp naturally aspirated. Bolting on an aftermarket supercharger kit can easily push this forged block past 700+ hp, limited only by the MCT transmission clutches and cooling capacity.)",
    fuelConsumption: {
      city: "18.0 - 22.0 L/100km",
      highway: "11.0 - 13.0 L/100km",
      mixed: "14.0 - 16.5 L/100km",
      sporty: "25.0+ L/100km"
    },
    oilSpecification: "8.5L 5W-40 or 0W-40 Synthetic (MB 229.5 strictly required. Frequent 5 000 km oil changes are absolutely critical to protect the camshaft lobes. The engine consumes up to 1L of oil per 1 000 km by design.)",
    commonIssues: [
      "Camshaft Lobe & Lifter Wear: The top end starves for oil during cold starts. Even with the upgraded SLS lifters, the M156 camshaft lobes are notoriously soft. If the oil is not perfectly maintained, or the car is revved hard while cold, the lobes will grind themselves into powder, causing a harsh ticking noise that requires camshaft replacement.",
      "PCV Valve Failure: The crankcase ventilation valve located at the rear of the intake manifold fails frequently. The internal diaphragm tears, causing the engine to consume massive amounts of oil, triggering Check Engine lights for lean mixtures, and emitting a distinct high-pitched squeal or whistle at idle.",
      "Intake Manifold Gasket Leaks & Cracking: The dual-plenum magnesium intake manifold develops vacuum leaks at the central gasket or cracks internally due to extreme heat cycling. This causes rough idling, severe hesitation under load, and lean running conditions.",
      "Electronic Steering Lock (ESL/ELV) Failure: THE defining chassis flaw of the W204. The tiny electric motor inside the steering column lock burns out, completely immobilizing the vehicle. The key turns, but the car will not crank and the steering remains locked.",
      "Motor Mount Collapse: The immense torque and weight of the 6.2L V8 violently crushes the fluid-filled engine mounts. When they collapse, the engine block rests directly on the subframe, causing severe, chassis-shaking vibrations at idle and under load.",
      "Idler Pulley Shattering: The M156 utilizes several plastic idler pulleys on the serpentine belt drive. Under the immense stress of high RPMs (7 200 RPM), the plastic becomes brittle and shatters, throwing the belt and disabling the water pump and alternator."
    ],
    vulnerabilities: [
      "Catastrophic Engine Damage (Camshafts): Ignoring a loud ticking noise from the top end will eventually result in the camshaft lobes wearing completely flat. This sends metallic powder throughout the engine oil, potentially destroying the main bearings and requiring a full engine teardown.",
      "Immobilization (ESL/ELV Failure): A burnt-out steering lock motor will leave you completely stranded without warning. Repairing this at the dealership costs upwards of 1 500 EUR, though aftermarket emulators offer a permanent and cheaper fix.",
      "Economic Total Loss (Composite Brakes): The Edition 507 is equipped with massive, specialized AMG composite front brake rotors (with red calipers). Replacing these highly specialized two-piece rotors and pads is astronomically expensive, often exceeding 2 500 EUR per axle.",
      "Transmission Limp Mode: The intense heat from the V8 can fry the conductor plate speed sensors in the 7G-Tronic MCT transmission, locking the car into a single gear. Furthermore, the wet startup clutch (MCT) can overheat and slip if subjected to repeated, aggressive Launch Control starts.",
      "Catastrophic Engine Damage (Hydro-locking): If the fuel injectors stick open (a known failure point on high-mileage M156 engines), they will dump raw fuel into the cylinders while parked, causing the engine to hydrolock and bend connecting rods upon startup."
    ],
