    yearRange: "2018-2021",
    timingType: "Chain (Located at the FRONT of the engine block. The M264 engine heavily revised the timing architecture from the older M274. It utilizes a robust roller-type timing chain. Mercedes significantly improved the durability of the Variable Valve Timing (VVT) adjusters, making the catastrophic cold-start rattles of the previous generation much less common. However, it is still a complex chain system that requires pristine, frequent oil changes to maintain proper tensioner pressure.)",
    injectionType: "Direct Injection / Bosch MED 17.7.x (A state-of-the-art, ultra-high-pressure gasoline direct injection system. The engine-driven High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) injects fuel directly into the combustion chamber at pressures exceeding 200 bar. This results in incredible fuel atomization. However, because fuel no longer washes over the intake valves, the M264 still suffers from performance-robbing carbon buildup on the intake tract over time, requiring physical walnut blasting. Furthermore, European facelift models are equipped with a Gasoline Particulate Filter (GPF/OPF) to meet Euro 6d-TEMP emissions.)",
    engineLifespan: "250 000 - 350 000+ km (The all-aluminum M264 block is a highly refined and robust unit. Mercedes engineered this 1.5L variant with CONICSHAPE internal cylinder honing to reduce friction and wear. Its lifespan in the W205 facelift chassis is exceptional if maintained correctly. The direct injection system naturally dilutes the engine oil with microscopic amounts of gasoline, meaning extending oil changes past 10 000 km will cause bearing wear. If the complex PCV system and cooling modules are maintained, the core engine will last a very long time.)",
    turboType: "Turbocharged (Equipped with an advanced, fast-spooling twin-scroll exhaust gas turbocharger integrated closely with the exhaust manifold. This twin-scroll setup entirely eliminates traditional turbo lag, delivering peak 250 Nm torque remarkably early. Because this specific C 180 variant is software-restricted to just 156 horsepower, the turbocharger operates incredibly far below its thermal and mechanical limits.)",
    ecuType: "Bosch (A lightning-fast, highly complex engine management system designed to tightly control direct injection, turbocharging, and the CAMTRONIC variable valve lift system. The ECU actively monitors rail pressure, manages the electronic thermostat, and strictly monitors the GPF/OPF exhaust backpressure. Tuning potential is phenomenal via the OBD-II port.)",
    euroStandard: "Euro 6d-TEMP (Equipped with GPF/OPF)",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "220+ hp (The internal rotating assembly of the downsized 1.5-liter M264 is highly advanced. Because this specific block shares its core architecture with the higher-output C 200 (which pushes 184hp + EQ Boost), the stock internals are vastly overbuilt for its incredibly conservative 156hp rating. The true limitation for massive power is the flow capacity of the factory turbocharger, the flow limits of the high-pressure fuel pump, and the highly restrictive GPF exhaust filter.)",
    fuelConsumption: {
      city: "7.5 - 8.5 L/100km",
      highway: "5.0 - 6.2 L/100km",
      mixed: "6.2 - 7.2 L/100km",
      sporty: "9.5+ L/100km"
    },
    oilSpecification: "6.0L 5W-40 or 5W-30 Low-SAPS Synthetic (MB 229.51 or MB 229.52 STRICTLY required due to the Gasoline Particulate Filter / GPF. Using standard MB 229.5 will destroy the exhaust filter.)",
    commonIssues: [
      "GPF / OPF Sensor Failures: Facelift models feature a Gasoline Particulate Filter. The differential pressure sensors frequently fail, triggering Check Engine lights and Limp Mode.",
      "Water Pump Failure: The M264 water pump features a complex vacuum-controlled plastic shroud designed to block coolant flow for faster warm-up. This shroud can jam, or the pump bearings fail, causing rapid overheating and coolant leaks.",
      "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) Leaks: The mechanical fuel pump can leak internally, sending raw gasoline directly into the engine oil and destroying its lubricity.",
      "Intake Valve Carbon Buildup: Due to direct injection, the intake valves build up thick carbon deposits, causing rough idling, misfires, and noticeable power losses.",
      "PCV System Failure: The crankcase ventilation system fails, pushing raw oil into the intake tract and turbocharger, severely exacerbating the carbon buildup issue and clogging the GPF."
    ],
    vulnerabilities: [
      "Catastrophic Engine Damage (Fuel Dilution): If the High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) internal leak is ignored, the gasoline-thinned oil will rapidly lose its lubricating properties. This fails to protect the main and rod bearings, causing the engine to completely seize and requiring a highly expensive full replacement.",
      "Overheating (Water Pump/Thermostat): Sudden failure of the complex vacuum-actuated water pump shroud or the electronic thermostat will restrict coolant flow. If the driver ignores the temperature warnings, the aluminum block will overheat rapidly, potentially warping the cylinder head and blowing the head gasket.",
      "Exhaust Choking (GPF/OPF Clogging): European facelift models feature a highly sensitive Gasoline Particulate Filter. If driven exclusively in stop-and-go city traffic without reaching high exhaust temperatures, or if incorrect non-Low-SAPS oil is used, the filter will permanently clog, forcing Limp Mode and requiring an immensely expensive replacement.",
      "Economic Total Loss (Turbocharger Assembly): The electronic wastegate actuator on the twin-scroll turbocharger is highly vulnerable to extreme heat cycling. When the actuator fails, Mercedes-Benz often mandates replacing the entire turbocharger assembly rather than just the actuator, leading to repair bills easily exceeding 2 000 EUR.",
      "Severe Power Loss (Intake Carbon Buildup): Due to the direct injection architecture, fuel no longer washes the back of the intake valves. Over tens of thousands of kilometers, thick, rock-hard carbon deposits choke the intake tract, leading to violent cold-start misfires, rough idling, and a severe loss of throttle response until physically walnut blasted.",
      "Transmission Limp Mode (9G-Tronic Neglect): The highly advanced 725.0 9-speed automatic transmission requires incredibly strict fluid changes at 90 000 km using specialized gold ATF and a new integrated plastic pan. Neglecting this service causes the complex internal valve body to stick, resulting in harsh shifts, Limp Mode, and potentially a 3 000+ EUR rebuild."
    ],
