    yearRange: "2014-2018",
    timingType: "Chain (Located at the REAR of the engine block. The M276 DES engine utilizes an incredibly complex multi-chain system. While fundamentally more robust than the older M272, early production models (pre-2015) suffer from secondary chain tensioner failures. The lack of oil pressure at startup causes the chain to slap against the guides, creating a loud 3-5 second metallic rattle. Mercedes issued a massive Technical Service Bulletin to install non-return check valves in the tensioners to hold oil pressure. If you hear the rattle, the repair is extremely expensive due to the incredibly tight V6 packaging in the W205.)",
    injectionType: "Direct Injection / Bosch MED 17.7.3 (A highly advanced, ultra-high-pressure gasoline direct injection system utilizing fast-acting piezoelectric injectors. The engine-driven High-Pressure Fuel Pumps (HPFP) inject fuel directly into the combustion chamber. However, because fuel no longer washes over the intake valves, the M276 suffers from severe, performance-robbing carbon buildup on the intake tract, requiring physical walnut blasting every 100 000 kilometers. The high-pressure pumps are also known to wear and leak internally over high mileages.)",
    engineLifespan: "250 000 - 350 000+ km (The all-aluminum 3.0L BiTurbo M276 block is a masterpiece of engineering. It features NANOSLIDE cylinder wall coating which practically eliminates piston friction and wear. However, its lifespan in the W205 chassis is entirely dictated by strict maintenance. The direct injection system naturally dilutes the engine oil with microscopic amounts of gasoline, meaning extending oil changes past 10 000 km will cause catastrophic bearing wear. If the timing chain tensioners are updated, the cooling system is maintained, and the oil changed religiously, the core engine is bulletproof.)",
    turboType: "Twin-Turbocharged (Equipped with two advanced, fast-spooling IHI turbochargers, one mounted directly to each cylinder bank. This setup entirely eliminates traditional turbo lag, delivering 480 Nm of torque incredibly early in the rev range. The M276 BiTurbo runs relatively low factory boost pressures to achieve its 333hp rating, meaning the turbochargers operate incredibly far below their thermal and mechanical limits. They are generally highly reliable, though vacuum lines controlling the wastegates can crack from heat.)",
    ecuType: "Bosch MED 17.7.3 (A lightning-fast, highly complex engine management system designed to tightly control the dual turbos and piezoelectric direct injection. The ECU actively monitors rail pressure, manages the electronic thermostat, and controls the smart alternator. Because this 3.0L block is identical to the 367hp C 43 AMG (pre-V8) and C 450 AMG models, the 333hp C 400 is artificially restricted by software, making its tuning potential absolutely phenomenal via the OBD-II port.)",
    euroStandard: "Euro 6",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "450+ hp (The internal rotating assembly of the 3.0-liter BiTurbo M276 is incredibly advanced and vastly overbuilt. Because this specific block was engineered to withstand high torque in the C 43 AMG, the stock internals handle the 333hp rating effortlessly. The engine can easily be tuned to over 400 horsepower with just a software flash. Pushing past 450hp safely requires upgraded downpipes and cooling modifications.)",
    fuelConsumption: {
      city: "10.5 - 12.5 L/100km",
      highway: "7.0 - 8.5 L/100km",
      mixed: "8.5 - 10.0 L/100km",
      sporty: "13.0+ L/100km"
    },
    oilSpecification: "6.5L 5W-40 Synthetic (MB 229.5 strictly required. Checking the oil dipstick weekly for the smell of gasoline is absolutely mandatory on direct injection models.)",
    commonIssues: [
      "Timing Chain Tensioner Rattle: A lack of check valves in early tensioners causes a loud 3-5 second metallic rattle on cold starts as oil drains out overnight. Requires updated tensioners and check valves to prevent chain stretch.",
      "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) Wear: The twin mechanical fuel pumps can wear internally, sending raw gasoline directly into the engine oil and destroying its lubricity.",
      "Intake Valve Carbon Buildup: Due to the direct injection architecture, fuel no longer washes the back of the intake valves. Over tens of thousands of kilometers, thick, rock-hard carbon deposits choke the intake tract, leading to violent cold-start misfires and power loss.",
      "PCV System Failure: The crankcase ventilation system fails internally, pushing raw oil vapor into the intake tract and turbochargers. This severely exacerbates the intake carbon buildup issue, causes increased oil consumption, and can foul the spark plugs.",
      "Water Pump & Thermostat Failure: The mechanical water pump and the complex electronic thermostat frequently fail, causing rapid overheating or severe cooling anomalies.",
      "4MATIC Transfer Case Binding: The C 400 is exclusively all-wheel drive. The integrated transfer case inside the 7G-Tronic requires specific fluid changes. Neglect causes binding and severe vibration during tight turns."
    ],
    vulnerabilities: [
      "Catastrophic Engine Damage (Timing Chain Snapping): If the cold start chain rattle is ignored, the loose chain will slap the plastic guides, breaking them. The debris will clog the oil pump, or the chain will skip teeth, causing pistons to smash into valves.",
      "Catastrophic Engine Damage (Fuel Dilution): If the High-Pressure Fuel Pumps leak internally, the gasoline-thinned oil will rapidly lose its lubricating properties. This fails to protect the main and rod bearings, causing the engine to completely seize.",
      "Overheating (Water Pump/Thermostat): Sudden failure of the water pump or the electronic thermostat will restrict coolant flow. If the driver ignores the temperature warnings, the aluminum block will overheat rapidly, potentially warping the cylinder heads.",
      "Severe Power Loss (Intake Carbon Buildup): Due to the direct injection architecture, fuel no longer washes the back of the intake valves. Over tens of thousands of kilometers, thick, rock-hard carbon deposits choke the intake tract, leading to a severe loss of throttle response until physically walnut blasted.",
      "Transmission Limp Mode (7G-Tronic Neglect): The 722.9 7-speed transmission must handle the V6 torque and house the 4MATIC system. It requires incredibly strict fluid changes every 60 000 km using specialized low-friction blue fluid to prevent conductor plate failure.",
      "Transfer Case Destruction (4MATIC Neglect): Failing to change the transfer case fluid will cause the internal clutch packs to bind. The resulting vibration will eventually destroy the transfer case, requiring a highly expensive replacement."
    ],
