    author: ".",
    popupAnimationName: "",
    drivingExperience: "The driving experience of the W206 C 300 de Plug-in Hybrid is a highly polarized blend of serene, silent electric luxury and violently abrupt combustion aggression. Mercedes massively upgraded the battery to 25.4 kWh for this generation. When charged, the car glides through city streets in pure EV mode with 129 horsepower and 440 Nm of instant electric torque, providing an incredibly peaceful, S-Class-like isolation for up to 100 kilometers. However, when you demand full power, the 2.0-liter turbocharged combustion engine violently kicks to life, immediately coupling its 197 horsepower to the drivetrain. This combined 313 hp and an absolute massive 700 Nm of torque catapults the car forward with sports-car rivaling thrust. The 9-speed hybrid transmission attempts to juggle these two power sources, but the transition can occasionally feel clunky. When the battery is fully depleted, you are left carrying hundreds of kilograms of dead battery weight, relying solely on the 2.0L engine, which significantly blunts performance and ruins fuel economy. It is a deeply complex, incredibly fast executive commuter designed primarily for extreme tax incentives and zero-emission urban zones.",
    mechanicVerdict: "The OM654M combustion engine within the W206 C 300 de Plug-in Hybrid earns a terrifying 9/10 risk rating due to its operating conditions. Mechanically, the core 2.0L aluminum block is robust, but it is subjected to horrific, highly unnatural abuse by the hybrid system. The internal combustion engine is constantly violently engaged and disengaged while driving, meaning it frequently hits full boost while completely cold. This extreme thermal shock severely accelerates wear on the head gasket, the turbocharger, and the rear-mounted timing chain. Furthermore, the massive 25.4 kWh high-voltage lithium-ion battery dictates the lifespan of the entire vehicle. Replacing this dead hybrid battery out of warranty can cost upwards of 8 000 to 12 000 EUR, often exceeding the car's residual value. The high-voltage inverter, electric A/C compressor, and dedicated hybrid cooling pumps are all prone to sudden, immobilizing failures. You still face all the standard OM654M issues, including terrifying dual-dosing AdBlue system failures and NOx sensor faults. Driving this PHEV exclusively in the city on the engine will guarantee severe DPF and EGR clogging issues. Because the timing chain is located at the rear of the engine, replacement requires taking the entire transmission or engine out. The massively heavy 2.1-ton weight of the vehicle crushes suspension control arms and tires much faster than a standard model. If you lease this car under warranty, the massive 700 Nm of combined torque makes it a brilliant driving experience. Tuning potential exists for this 197hp combustion engine, but modifying the hybrid power delivery is extremely dangerous for the 9-speed gearbox. Once the high-voltage battery warranty expires, this complex PHEV vehicle represents a catastrophic financial liability. It should be entirely avoided on the used market unless you possess an unlimited repair budget.",
    repairCostEstimate: "$2,000 - $8,000+ / year (If the high-voltage battery fails, replacement costs 8,000 - 12,000 EUR. Replacing the Thermal Management Module costs ~400 EUR. Hybrid inverter or electric motor failures will cost thousands. NOx sensors/AdBlue issues cost 1,000+ EUR. A clogged DPF replacement costs over 2,000 EUR.)"
